---
// Privacy policy for the phux mobile apps (iOS, Android), phux.sh, and the
// hosted demo. The apps link here from the paywall and account screens
// (TUIST_PRIVACY_POLICY_URL in phux-mobile). Every statement is derived from
// code: the site workers (host/, worker/src/), the private analytics worker
// that host/analytics.ts forwards to, and the phux-mobile sources and ADRs.
// When any of those change what is collected, change this page in the same
// release and bump LAST_UPDATED.
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const LAST_UPDATED = "2026-09-23";
const CONTACT = "privacy@phall.io";
const title = "phux — privacy policy";
const description =
  "What the phux apps, phux.sh, and the hosted demo collect, who processes it, how long it is kept, and how to access or delete it.";
---

<BaseLayout {title} {description}>
  <article class="wrap policy">
    <header class="policy-head">
      <h1>privacy policy</h1>
      <p class="updated">
        Last updated <time datetime={LAST_UPDATED}>{LAST_UPDATED}</time>
      </p>
      <p class="lede">
        This policy covers the phux mobile apps for iOS and Android, the
        websites at <code>phux.sh</code>, <code>docs.phux.sh</code>, and <code>shell.phux.sh</code>,
        and the phux software you install and run yourself. "We" means the people who operate phux and these services.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="summary" aria-labelledby="summary">
      <h2 id="summary">in short</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Your terminal traffic goes directly between your device and the phux
          server you run. We never receive, store, or process terminal content.
        </li>
        <li>
          The apps work without an account. If you create one, we store your
          email address and the settings you choose to sync, never pairing
          tokens or terminal data.
        </li>
        <li>
          Purchases are handled by Apple or Google. RevenueCat manages
          subscription status under a random ID, not your email address.
        </li>
        <li>
          The website keeps aggregate traffic counts and a hashed per-day
          visitor ID. Its analytics never store IP addresses, and it sets no
          tracking cookie unless you opt in.
        </li>
        <li>
          No advertising, no selling of data, and no tracking across other
          companies' apps or websites.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="terminals">
      <h2 id="terminals">your terminals</h2>
      <p>
        The apps connect to a phux server that you install and run on your own
        machine. The connection goes directly from your device to that
        server: over your local network, over an overlay network such as
        Tailscale or WireGuard, or through a relay you host yourself. We do
        not operate a relay, and no phux-operated service sits in that path.
        Terminal output, scrollback, the commands you type, and session titles
        never reach us.
      </p>
      <p>
        When you pair a device with a server, the pairing token and
        certificate fingerprint are stored on your device (in the iOS
        Keychain, or encrypted with the Android Keystore). Pairing tokens are
        never synced to your account and never leave your device except to
        authenticate to your own server.
      </p>
      <p>
        The phux command-line tool and server contain no telemetry or usage
        reporting. They contact GitHub only to download a release, when you
        run the installer or <code>phux update</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="device">
      <h2 id="device">what stays on your device</h2>
      <p>
        Saved servers, preferences, and app settings are stored locally. The
        apps include no analytics or crash-reporting SDK. Specific device
        features are used only when you invoke them:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Camera</strong>: to scan a pairing QR code. On Android,
          scanning uses the Google Play services code scanner, and the app
          receives only the decoded link.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Microphone and speech recognition</strong> (iOS): to dictate
          a message. Transcription runs on your device.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Face ID</strong> (iOS): to unlock the app when you turn on
          the optional app lock.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Local network</strong>: to reach your phux server.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Notifications that a session needs your attention are generated on
        your device. The apps are not set up to receive push notifications.
      </p>
      <p>
        On iOS, the app keeps Apple's MetricKit performance and hang reports
        in its own storage, capped at the 60 most recent files and excluded
        from backups. They contain timing and system diagnostics, not terminal
        content, and are not uploaded. The app's support report is sent only
        if you share it yourself. You can read it before you share it, and it
        excludes server names, tokens, account data, and terminal content.
      </p>
      <p>
        On Android, if you have turned on device backup, Android may include
        the app's local data in your Google account backup. That backup is
        between you and Google.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="account">
      <h2 id="account">optional account</h2>
      <p>
        An account is optional. Its purpose is to sync your settings across
        devices. It is hosted on Supabase. You can sign in with Apple (iOS) or
        with a one-time code sent to your email address. There are no
        passwords.
      </p>
      <p>When you have an account, we store:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          your email address (with Sign in with Apple, this can be an Apple
          private relay address) and a random account ID;
        </li>
        <li>
          if you use Sign in with Apple, an Apple-issued refresh token, kept
          only so that we can revoke Apple's sign-in grant when you delete
          your account;
        </li>
        <li>
          the settings you sync: server names and addresses, certificate
          fingerprints, workspace names (and any tailnet name you attach to a
          workspace), session collection names and the panes they contain,
          per-server input and font settings, and app preferences such as the
          key bar layout, font size, and haptics;
        </li>
        <li>
          your commerce ID (see <a href="#purchases">purchases</a>), so that a
          subscription follows you to a new device.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We do not store pairing tokens, terminal content, or anything you type
        into a terminal. Signing out only affects the device you sign out on:
        its local data stays, and syncing stops.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Deleting your account.</strong> In the iOS app, open the
        account screen and tap "delete account". Deleting your account:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>revokes the Apple sign-in grant, if you used Sign in with Apple;</li>
        <li>deletes your RevenueCat customer record;</li>
        <li>
          deletes your account, your email address, and every synced document
          from our Supabase database;
        </li>
        <li>
          removes the commerce ID from the device and resets its subscription
          state.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Deleting your account does not cancel an App Store or Google Play
        subscription. You cancel those in your Apple or Google subscription
        settings. If your version of the app does not offer in-app deletion,
        email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a> and we will delete
        your account and associated data.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="purchases">
      <h2 id="purchases">purchases</h2>
      <p>
        Subscriptions bought in the app are sold by Apple (App Store) or
        Google (Google Play), who process payment. We never see your card or
        payment details.
      </p>
      <p>
        We use RevenueCat to track which subscriptions are active. The app
        creates a random commerce ID on your device and uses it as your
        RevenueCat customer ID. It is not your email address, a device
        identifier, or a pairing token. RevenueCat receives:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>the commerce ID;</li>
        <li>
          your purchase and subscription transactions from Apple or Google;
        </li>
        <li>
          technical details that its SDK sends with each request: platform,
          operating system version, device model, app version, preferred
          languages, and store country. On iOS, this also includes Apple's
          identifier for vendor, an ID that is specific to our apps on your
          device and cannot be used to link you across other companies' apps.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We do not send RevenueCat your email address, name, server
        information, or anything from your terminals. Where web purchases are
        offered, Paddle processes them as merchant of record, and RevenueCat
        links them to the same commerce ID.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="website">
      <h2 id="website">the website</h2>
      <p>
        <code>phux.sh</code> and <code>docs.phux.sh</code> run on Cloudflare.
        The site loads no third-party analytics script and sets no cookies for
        ordinary visits. Its fonts load from Bunny Fonts, which receives your
        IP address as part of that request. Two kinds of measurement run on
        the server side.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Public aggregate counters.</strong> For each request, the site
        increments hourly counters by user-agent class (for example
        "browser" or a named AI crawler), country, hostname, response status,
        and type of request. These counters contain no IP addresses, raw user
        agents, or cookies. They are published live at <a href="/telemetry">/telemetry</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Private analytics.</strong> Each request is also recorded in a
        private event store. It stores the hostname and page path (never the
        query string, except the campaign tags <code>utm_source</code>, <code>utm_medium</code>, and <code>utm_campaign</code>),
        the referring site's hostname, country, and user-agent class, and for
        installer downloads the operating system, CPU architecture, and
        download tool. Visitors are counted
        with an ID computed from your IP address and user agent under a key
        that changes every day, so visits cannot be linked from one day to
        the next. The IP address itself is never stored. To tell people apart
        from AI agents, crawlers, and scanners, a summary of a visitor's day
        (user-agent class, country, referrer type, campaign tags, and pages
        visited; for unrecognized clients, a truncated user-agent string with
        email addresses removed) can be sent to TypeSafe, an AI
        classification service. The summary never includes an IP address,
        email address, or cookie.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The beta list.</strong> If you <a href="/#beta">join the beta</a>,
        we store your email address, the
        date you joined, the form you used, your country, and your user-agent
        class. We use it to email you about the beta. We do not sell or rent
        it. The beta emails include a personal link. If you choose to open it,
        it sets a first-party <code>phux_mid</code> cookie for one year, which
        links your later visits on that browser to your beta membership. This
        cookie is the only way the site identifies an individual visitor, and
        only happens if you click that link.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The hosted shell.</strong> The live terminal on the landing
        page runs on <code>shell.phux.sh</code>. The basic version needs no
        sign-in. To unlock the full Linux shell, you sign in with GitHub or
        Google. From GitHub we read your public user ID, username, and account
        creation date. From Google we read your account ID, name, and verified
        email address. Your identity is kept in a signed cookie that expires
        after 8 hours. On our side, only your provider account ID is kept, to
        enforce usage limits: launch records are deleted after one hour and
        daily usage totals at the end of the UTC day. Your IP address is used
        to rate-limit launches. It is stored in keyed-hash form for rate
        limiting, and in plain form only while a hosted session is running.
        Hosted sessions are discarded when they end.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pairing links.</strong> When a <code>phux.sh/connect</code> pairing link opens in a browser instead of the app, the page reads the
        link on your device and removes the token from the address bar. The
        page loads no analytics script, and our analytics record only the
        path, never the token.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="never">
      <h2 id="never">what we do not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We do not show advertising or use advertising identifiers.</li>
        <li>We do not sell or rent personal data.</li>
        <li>
          We do not track you across other companies' apps or websites. The
          iOS app's privacy manifest declares no tracking and no tracking
          domains.
        </li>
        <li>We do not use your data to build advertising profiles.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="providers">
      <h2 id="providers">service providers</h2>
      <p>
        These companies process data for us, only for the purposes described
        above. They may process it in the United States and other countries.
      </p>
      <div class="table-region" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Service providers">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Provider</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Apple, Google</td><td>App distribution, in-app purchases, Sign in with Apple, Google sign-in for the hosted shell</td></tr>
            <tr><td>RevenueCat</td><td>Subscription status</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Supabase</td><td>Optional account and settings sync</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Paddle</td><td>Web purchases, where offered</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cloudflare</td><td>Website hosting, hosted shell, website analytics storage</td></tr>
            <tr><td>GitHub</td><td>Sign-in for the hosted shell, software downloads</td></tr>
            <tr><td>TypeSafe</td><td>Classifying website traffic</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Bunny Fonts</td><td>Serving the website's fonts</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="retention">
      <h2 id="retention">how long we keep it</h2>
      <div class="table-region" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Retention periods">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Data</th><th scope="col">Kept for</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Account, email, synced settings</td><td>Until you delete your account</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Removed servers in the synced roster</td><td>30 days, so the removal reaches your other devices</td></tr>
            <tr><td>RevenueCat customer record</td><td>Until you delete your account or ask us to delete it</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Public website counters</td><td>30 days</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Private website events</td><td>90 days</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Daily visitor records and traffic classifications</td><td>45 days</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Beta list</td><td>Until you ask to be removed</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Hosted shell sign-in cookie</td><td>8 hours</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Hosted shell launch records and usage totals</td><td>1 hour; until the end of the UTC day</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Apple, Google, and Paddle keep their own purchase records under their
        own policies.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="rights">
      <h2 id="rights">your choices and rights</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Access and export.</strong> The iOS app can export
          everything your account holds as a JSON file: open the account
          screen and tap "export account data". On other platforms, or for
          other data, email us.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Deletion.</strong> Delete your account in the app as
          described above, or email us. To leave the beta list, email us.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Correction.</strong> You can change synced settings in the
          app. For anything else, email us.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No account.</strong> All terminal features work without an
          account.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Depending on where you live, you may have further rights, such as
        objecting to or restricting processing, or complaining to a data
        protection authority. Email us to exercise any of them. We will not
        treat you differently for doing so.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="children">
      <h2 id="children">children</h2>
      <p>
        phux is a developer tool. It is not directed at children under 13,
        and we do not knowingly collect personal data from them. If you
        believe a child has given us personal data, email us and we will
        delete it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="changes">
      <h2 id="changes">changes to this policy</h2>
      <p>
        When we change this policy, we update the date at the top of this
        page. If a change affects how we use data you have already given us,
        we will say so on this page before the change takes effect. The
        history of this page is public in the <a href="https://github.com/no-phux/phux/commits/main/docs/site/src/pages/privacy.astro" target="_blank" rel="noopener">phux repository</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="contact">
      <h2 id="contact">contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions and requests about privacy: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .policy {
    max-width: calc(var(--reading-width) + 2 * var(--page-gutter));
    padding-block: clamp(32px, 6vw, 72px) 96px;
  }
  .policy-head {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    padding-bottom: 24px;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }
  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(28px, 4vw, 40px);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin: 0 0 8px;
  }
  .updated {
    font-family: var(--font-code);
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    margin: 0 0 16px;
  }
  .lede {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    margin: 0;
  }
  section {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    padding-block: 24px;
  }
  section:last-child {
    border-bottom: 0;
  }
  h2 {
    font-size: 20px;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    margin: 0 0 12px;
  }
  p,
  ul {
    margin: 0 0 12px;
  }
  ul {
    padding-left: 1.25em;
  }
  li + li {
    margin-top: 6px;
  }
  .summary {
    border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    padding: 20px;
    margin-block: 24px;
  }
  .summary ul {
    margin: 0;
  }
  a {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }
  a:hover,
  a:focus-visible {
    text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
  }
  code {
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }
  .table-region {
    max-width: 100%;
    overflow-x: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    margin: 0 0 12px;
  }
  .table-region:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }
  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 14px;
  }
  th,
  td {
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    padding: 10px 12px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
  }
  tbody tr:last-child td {
    border-bottom: 0;
  }
  th {
    background: var(--color-surface-raised);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
